WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.230
What is actually essential for offline
reinforcement learning done via supervised learning?

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.040
This ICLR 2022 paper by Scott Emmons, Benjamin
Eysenbach, Ilya Kostrikov,

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.850
and Sergey Levine strips reinforcement learning via
supervised learning, or RvS,

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.660
down to its bare essentials.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.470
Their surprising finding is that a plain two-layer
feedforward network,

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.280
trained simply to maximize likelihood,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.090
matches state-of-the-art results from far more
complex methods built on temporal-difference

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:30.900
learning or on Transformer sequence models.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.710
The two things that really matter, they show,

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.520
are choosing the model's capacity carefully and
choosing what to condition on,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.330
goals or rewards.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:46.140
Everything else turns out to be optional.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.563
Recent work showed that plain supervised learning,

00:00:49.643 --> 00:00:52.623
with no temporal-difference bootstrapping at all,

00:00:52.703 --> 00:00:56.558
can be remarkably effective for offline
reinforcement learning.

00:00:56.638 --> 00:00:58.182
But the picture was muddy.

00:00:58.262 --> 00:01:03.803
Different papers reached contradictory conclusions
about what actually makes these methods

00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:06.426
work: some emphasized advantage weighting,

00:01:06.506 --> 00:01:09.736
others reached for large Transformer sequence models.

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:13.859
The core question this paper asks is simple, yet
still unanswered.

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:17.544
When does supervised learning for offline RL
actually work,

00:01:17.624 --> 00:01:23.102
and which of its many algorithmic components are
truly essential versus merely incidental

00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:26.350
complexity that could be stripped away without cost?

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.097
Value-based methods dominate offline and off-policy RL,

00:01:30.177 --> 00:01:33.356
and they come with appealing theoretical guarantees.

00:01:33.436 --> 00:01:36.113
But in practice they are difficult to apply.

00:01:36.193 --> 00:01:42.694
They require complex tricks to stabilize learning
and careful tuning of many interacting hyperparameters.

00:01:42.774 --> 00:01:48.585
An attractive alternative is to convert the
reinforcement learning problem into a conditional,

00:01:48.665 --> 00:01:51.656
filtered, or weighted imitation learning problem,

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:57.484
using the insight that experience that is
suboptimal for one task may be optimal for another.

00:01:57.564 --> 00:02:02.185
If a minimal supervised recipe can match these
complex value-based methods,

00:02:02.265 --> 00:02:05.506
it would give practitioners a dependable field guide,

00:02:05.586 --> 00:02:10.520
and it would also reveal exactly where such
supervised methods still break down.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.935
The paper makes three contributions.

00:02:15.015 --> 00:02:19.049
First, it does not propose a brand-new algorithm;

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:23.164
instead it places many existing goal-conditioned
and reward-conditioned methods under one common framework,

00:02:23.244 --> 00:02:27.278
which the authors call RvS, reinforcement learning
via supervised learning.

00:02:27.358 --> 00:02:31.393
Second,

00:02:31.473 --> 00:02:35.507
through extensive experiments it boils these
methods down to their essential elements,

00:02:35.587 --> 00:02:39.622
showing that a two-layer feedforward network
trained to maximize likelihood is competitive

00:02:39.702 --> 00:02:43.736
with far more complex state-of-the-art methods.

00:02:43.816 --> 00:02:47.851
Third, it identifies exactly which design choices
matter, namely model capacity,

00:02:47.931 --> 00:02:51.965
regularization, and what you condition on, and it
honestly probes the limits,

00:02:52.045 --> 00:02:56.080
showing that RvS is comparatively weak on purely
random data.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.544
The method is deliberately simple.

00:02:58.624 --> 00:03:04.655
RvS assumes an agent in a Markov decision process
and trains a policy conditioned on an outcome,

00:03:04.735 --> 00:03:08.983
which can be either a future goal state or an
average future return.

00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:11.593
Given an offline dataset of trajectories,

00:03:11.673 --> 00:03:17.386
it applies hindsight relabeling: every observed
action becomes a demonstration for whatever

00:03:17.466 --> 00:03:20.950
outcome actually occurred later in that same trajectory.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:26.934
The policy itself is just a feedforward multilayer
perceptron with two fully connected layers,

00:03:27.014 --> 00:03:31.644
and the outcome is fed in simply by concatenating
it onto the input state.

00:03:31.724 --> 00:03:37.755
Training then maximizes the log-likelihood of the
observed actions under the conditioned policy.

00:03:37.835 --> 00:03:42.210
There is no advantage weighting, no
temporal-difference bootstrapping,

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:47.430
and no Transformer, only a maximum-likelihood
objective over relabeled experience.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.215
The evaluation is deliberately broad.

00:03:50.295 --> 00:03:54.311
On the D4RL benchmark, the authors use three
suites: AntMaze,

00:03:54.391 --> 00:03:59.080
which requires an eight-degree-of-freedom quadruped
to navigate a maze;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.116
Gym Locomotion, with HalfCheetah, Hopper, and
Walker across random, medium,

00:04:04.196 --> 00:04:08.280
medium-replay, and medium-expert datasets; and
Franka Kitchen,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.317
a nine-degree-of-freedom manipulation task built
from human demonstrations.

00:04:13.397 --> 00:04:17.011
They also use the GCSL suite of goal-conditioned tasks,

00:04:17.091 --> 00:04:21.779
including two-dimensional navigation, Sawyer arm
control, Lunar Lander,

00:04:21.859 --> 00:04:23.055
and a robotic claw,

00:04:23.135 --> 00:04:27.890
which they adapt for offline RL by collecting data
with a random policy.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:34.270
All scores are normalized into a
zero-to-one-hundred range so methods can be compared directly.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:36.812
The headline result is striking.

00:04:36.892 --> 00:04:42.556
Using nothing but a two-layer feedforward network
trained with maximum likelihood,

00:04:42.636 --> 00:04:46.970
RvS reaches state-of-the-art performance across
several suites.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:52.154
On AntMaze, goal-conditioned RvS scores fifty-three
point five on average,

00:04:52.234 --> 00:04:56.357
edging out the best value-based baseline at fifty
point six.

00:04:56.437 --> 00:04:59.160
On Franka Kitchen it reaches fifty-four.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:05.114
On the GCSL suite it scores sixty-two, beating the
online GCSL method at fifty-eight,

00:05:05.194 --> 00:05:07.356
despite using only offline data.

00:05:07.436 --> 00:05:08.897
And on Gym Locomotion,

00:05:08.977 --> 00:05:15.483
reward-conditioned RvS matches Decision Transformer
while using a simple multilayer perceptron

00:05:15.563 --> 00:05:17.654
instead of a large Transformer.

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:22.418
Even on stitching tasks, long thought to demand
dynamic programming,

00:05:22.498 --> 00:05:24.660
goal-conditioned RvS keeps pace.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.938
The ablations pin down what actually matters.

00:05:29.018 --> 00:05:32.917
First,

00:05:32.997 --> 00:05:36.895
capacity: the best architectures are notably larger
than those used in standard online RL or imitation learning,

00:05:36.975 --> 00:05:40.874
and widening the network up to about a thousand
hidden units generally helps.

00:05:40.954 --> 00:05:44.852
Second, regularization: dropout is not universally good.

00:05:44.932 --> 00:05:48.831
It boosts performance on the small,
human-demonstration kitchen-complete dataset,

00:05:48.911 --> 00:05:52.809
but it hurts on hopper-medium-expert and on
antmaze-medium-play.

00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:56.788
Third,

00:05:56.868 --> 00:06:00.766
the output distribution: a categorical distribution
over discretized actions matches or

00:06:00.846 --> 00:06:04.745
beats a unimodal Gaussian across the GCSL tasks,

00:06:04.825 --> 00:06:08.723
which fits the broader theme that more policy
capacity helps.

00:06:08.803 --> 00:06:12.702
Finally, validation loss correlates only loosely
with final performance,

00:06:12.782 --> 00:06:16.680
so it is not a reliable tuning signal on its own.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:20.612
The takeaway is essentially a practitioner's field guide.

00:06:20.692 --> 00:06:26.538
You do not need advantage weighting or a
Transformer to do offline RL by supervised learning.

00:06:26.618 --> 00:06:31.444
A plain two-layer feedforward network, trained
simply to maximize likelihood,

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:34.057
is competitive with the state of the art,

00:06:34.137 --> 00:06:40.174
as long as you get two things right: carefully tune
the model's capacity and its regularization,

00:06:40.254 --> 00:06:44.061
and choose the right thing to condition on, goals
or rewards.

00:06:44.141 --> 00:06:48.968
The concrete recipe is to grow the network width
until performance saturates,

00:06:49.048 --> 00:06:50.624
then add a little dropout.

00:06:50.704 --> 00:06:53.683
The honest caveat is that on purely random data,

00:06:53.763 --> 00:06:56.104
temporal-difference methods still win,

00:06:56.184 --> 00:06:59.800
which the authors flag as an open problem for future work.
